# CH <sub/>3</sub> NH <sub/>3</sub> PbBr <sub/>3</sub> -CH <sub/>3</sub> NH <sub/>3</sub> PbI <sub/>3</sub> Perovskite-Perovskite Tandem Solar Cells with Exceeding 2.2 V Open Circuit Voltage

## abstract
Perovskite-perovskite tandem solar cells with open-circuit voltages of over 2.2 V are reported. These cost-effective, solution-processible perovskite hybrid tandem solar cells with high open-circuit voltages are fabricated by the simple lamination of a front planar MAPbBr3 perovskite cell and a back MAPbI3 planar perovskite solar cell.

CH NH PbBr –CH NH PbI  Perovskite–Perovskite Tandem Solar Cells with Exceeding 2.2 V Open Circuit Voltage
Jin Hyuck Heo and Sang Hyuk Im\*
Solar energy has been considered as a promising candidate for replacing conventional fossil fuels because it is clean, renewable, sustainable, and abundant enough to satisfy energy demand for human beings on the earth. Solar cells are useful photovoltaic devices to directly convert the solar light to electricity because the electricity is the most convenient and safe energy form in our daily life. However, the solar cells can only generate the electricity under illumination of day light but not generate any energy in night time. Many researchers have studied to convert the solar light to chemical energy in order to store and exploit the solar energy at any time. One of the simplest products in solar fuel is hydrogen which can be produced by direct water-electrolysis under applied bias voltage ${>}1.23\mathrm{~V~}$ or by water-splitting through photocatalysts. To efficiently generate solar fuels from solar cells, it is crucial to attain high open circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{oc}})$ to overcome the overpotential required in practical solar fuel generation systems. 1
To date, the perovskite hybrid solar cells have been of great interest because of their unique material properties such as high absorptivity by direct band gap, convenient band gap tailoring by simple combination/mixing of perovskite materials, long diffusion length of charge carriers, high open circuit voltage by small exciton binding energy, and low temperature solution processibility. Since Kojima et al. 2  reported $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b X_{3}}$ $\mathrm{(MAPbX_{3}}$ $\Chi=\operatorname{Br},\mathrm{~I~}$ ) perovskite-sensitized solar cells, the efficient mesoscopic, 3  planar, 4  and mesosuperstructure perovskite hybrid solar cells 5  have been intensively studied until now. Although the current record efficiency of perovskite hybrid solar cells is reached $>20\%$ , 3i  the solar cells work only in day time. Therefore, it is equally important to convert the solar energy to chemical energy such as solar fuels, which requires high potential energy. Here, we fabricated cost-effective, solution-processible perovskite hybrid tandem solar cells with high $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ by laminating front planar $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b B r_{3}}$ $({\mathrm{MAPb}}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3},$ ) perovskite cell and back $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ $(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3})$ planar perovskite solar cell.
In Figure 1  we show an illustration of the fabrication procedure of $\mathrm{MAPbBr}_{3}–\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ perovskite–perovskite hybrid tandem solar cell. For a front $\mathrm{MAPbBr}_{3}$ perovskite cell (Figure 1a), 4d we deposited ${\approx}50~\mathrm{nm}$ thick dense blocking $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{\Delta[bl-TiO_{2,}]}$ ) on a cleaned FTO (F-doped tin oxide) by spray pyrolysis deposition of $20\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{~M~}$ titanium diisopropoxide bis(acetylacetonate) solution at $450~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Then ${\approx}500~\mathrm{nm}$ thick pinhole-free $\mathrm{MAPbBr}_{3}$ perovskite thin film on the $\mathrm{\bl{-}T i O_{2}/F T O}$ substrate by spincoating of $40\mathrm{wt\%}$ $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ in DMF (N N-dimethylformamide) solution with HBr additive $(\mathrm{MAPbBr_{3}}/\mathrm{DMF};\mathrm{HBr}=1\$ mL:0.1 mL) at 3000 rpm for $200\mathrm{~s~}$ P3HT (poly-3-hexylthiophene) or PTAA (poly-triarylamine) hole conductor/toluene solution $(15~\mathrm{\mg/1}$ mL) with $7.5~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ Li-bis(trifluoromethanesulfonyl) imide (LiTFSI)/acetonitrile (ACN) $(170~\mathrm{mg/1~mL}$ ) and $7.5~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ t-BP (tertbutylpyrridine)/acetonitrile $(1~\mathrm{mL}/1~\mathrm{mL}$ additives was dropped on the $\mathrm{MAPbBr_{3}/b l{\cdot}T i O_{2}/F T O}$ substrate. For a single junction $\mathrm{MAPb}\mathrm{Br}_{3}$ planar perovskite hybrid solar cell (see a bottom scanning electron microscopy (SEM) cross-sectional image of Figure 1a), we spin-coated the hole conducting solutions at $3000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $30\mathrm{~s~}$ and an ${\approx}60~\mathrm{nm}$ thick Au counter electrode was thermally deposited by thermal evaporator. 4k
For a back $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ perovskite cell (Figure 1b), 4j  a 1:2 vol:vol PEDOT:PSS (poly(3,4-ethylenedioxythiophene):poly(styrenesulfonic acid)):methanol solution was spin-coated on a cleaned ITO (indium tin oxide) substrate at $3000\ \mathrm{rpm}$ for $60\mathrm{~s~}$ and dried at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $20~\mathrm{min}$ . $40\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ in DMF solution with HI additive $\mathrm{(MAPbI_{3}/D M F;H I=1\ m L;0.1\ m L)}$ was then spin-coated on the PEDOT:PSS/ITO substrate at $3000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $200\mathrm{~s~}$ and was dried on a hot plate at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 2 min. A phenyl-C61-butyric acid methyl ester (PCBM) layer was deposited on the $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3.}$ /PEDOT:PSS/ITO substrate by spin-coating PCBM/toluene $(20\mathrm{mg}/1\mathrm{mL}$ ) solution at $2000\ \mathrm{rpm}$ for $60~\mathrm{s}$ For a single junction $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ planar perovskite hybrid solar cell, we deposited ${\approx}60\ \mathrm{nm}$ thick Au counter electrode by thermal evaporation. 4j
For an $\mathrm{MAPbBr_{3}–M A P b I_{3}}$ tandem solar cell (Figure 1c), we simply laminated the $\mathrm{FTO/bl.TiO_{2}/M A P b B r_{3}/w e t}$ P3HT or PTAA substrate and the PCBM/MAPbI3/PEDOT:PSS/ITO substrate by sandwiching two substrates by pressurizing with double clip and subsequent drying. 6  The SEM cross-sectional image of $\mathrm{MAPbBr}_{3}–\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ tandem solar cell shows that the thickness of $\mathsf{b l}{\cdot}\mathrm{Ti}\mathsf{O}_{2}$ $\mathbf{MAPbBr}_{3}$ hole conductor, PCBM, $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ and PEDOT:PSS is ${\approx}50$ , ${\approx}500$ , ${\approx}2000$ , ${\approx}50$ , ${\approx}300$ , and ${\approx}50\ \mathrm{nm}$ , respectively. The bottom photograph is a front and back side image of representative $\mathrm{MAPbBr}_{3}–\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ tandem solar cell.
Upon illumination of light, the $\mathbf{MAPbBr}_{3}$ front cell and $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ back cell simultaneously generate loosely bonded electron–hole pairs or free charge carriers in $\mathrm{MAPbBr}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ light absorbing layer, respectively, due to small exciton binding energy: ${\approx}2\mathrm{kT}$ $(\approx50\ \mathrm{meV})$ for $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ and ${\approx}3\mathrm{kT}$ $(\approx75\ \mathrm{meV})$ for $\mathbf{MAPbBr}_{3}$ where $k$ is Boltzmann constant and $T$ is absolute temperature. 7  The generated electrons in $\mathrm{MAPb}\mathrm{Br}_{3}$ are transported to $\mathsf{b l{-}T i O}_{2}$ electron conductor and at the same time, the holes are transported to P3HT or PTAA hole transporting material (HTM). Similarly, the generated electrons in $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ are transported to PCBM electron conductor and the holes are transported to PEDOT:PSS hole conductor as well. If there is no efficient recombination zone (tunnel junction) between $\mathbf{MAPbBr}_{3}$ front and $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ back cell as illustrated in Figure 1d, the holes transported to P3HT or PTAA hole conductor and the electrons transported to PCBM cannot be effectively recombined in current tandem device forming thick hole conducting layer $({\approx}2000\ \mathrm{nm})$ so that the series tandem solar cells could not work well. 8  Namely, the holes transported from $\mathrm{MAPbBr}_{3}$ into P3HT or PTAA HTM without additives cannot be transported to the HOMO (highest occupied molecular orbital) of PCBM due to the energy barrier. Similarly, the electrons transported from $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ into PCBM electron conductor also cannot be transported to the LUMO (lowest unoccupied molecular orbital) of HTM due to the large energy barrier. Accordingly, the electrons generated in $\mathrm{MAPbI_{3}/P C B M}$ back cell cannot be transported to $\mathrm{HTM}/\mathrm{MAPbBr}_{3}$ front cell because the electrons are blocked at PCBM/HTM and the holes in $\mathrm{MAPbBr_{3}/H T M}$ front cell cannot be transported to $\mathrm{PCBM/MAPbI_{3}}$ back cell, as well. Accordingly, the blocked electrons and holes at HTM/ PCBM interface are doomed to be recombined in $\mathrm{MAPbBr}_{3}/$ HTM/PCBM/MAPbI3 layer through radiative or nonradiative recombination. If the holes generated in $\mathrm{MAPbBr}_{3}$ are not effectively transported to the HTM/PCBM interface due to very poor conductivity by thick HTM layer without additive, the hole extraction efficiency will be significantly suppressed and the survived holes can be recombined at HTM/PCBM interface. Therefore, it is expected that if we further reduce the thickness of HTM layer, the thinner HTM layer without additive can transport the holes more efficiently than the thicker HTM layer (see the result of Figure 2f). However, practically it is very difficult to form extremely thin HTM layer without additives while maintaining adhesiveness between $\mathrm{MAPb}\mathrm{Br}_{3}$ front cell and $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ back cell by lamination process. Accordingly, the relatively thick HTM layer is necessary to adhere the front and the back cell so that the success of series tandem is greatly dependent on the efficient transport of holes in the relatively thick HTM in order to increase $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of tandem device. Then we need to consider the short circuit current density $(J_{\mathrm{sc}})$ matching between front cell and back cell in order to maximize the device performance.
In the $\mathrm{FTO/bl{\cdot}T i O_{2}/M A P b B r_{3}/H T M}$ front cell, the electrons generated in $\mathrm{MAPbBr}_{3}$ perovskite are transported to the FTO by injection from $\mathbf{MAPbBr}_{3}$ into $\mathrm{~n~}$ -type bl- $\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and/or self-transporting through $\mathbf{MAPbBr}_{3}$ layer itself. Therefore, the electrons are efficiently transported to FTO electrode so that the conductive hole conductor can be used to transport holes. The single junction $\mathrm{ITO/PEDOT{:}P S S/M A P b I_{3}/P C B M/A u}$ back cell confirms that the planar perovskite hybrid solar cells work well by using conductive hole conductor (PEDOT:PSS). We have found that the HTM with Li-TFSI and $t{\mathrm{-BP}}$ additives act as conductive hole conductor like as quasi-solid electrolyte because the additives provide $\mathrm{Li/Li^{+}}$ redox shuttle. 3f  Consequently, the HTM with additives had ${\approx}40$ -fold increased conductivity. Similarly, the HTM with Li-TFSI and $t{\cdot}\mathrm{BP}$ additives (w/additives) will have more increased conductivity by the role as $\mathrm{Li/Li+}$ redox shuttle than the HTM without additives (w/o additives) as shown in Figure 1e. The more conductive HTM with additives can transport holes more efficiently to the HTM without additives/PCBM interface than the HTM without additives and the electrons in PCBM are also more efficiently recombined to the holes. Therefore, the HTM with additives can act as more efficient recombination layer (tunnel junction) than the HTM without additives and consequently high $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ can be built up by the simply sandwiched series tandem cell of front $\mathbf{MAPbBr}_{3}$ cell and back $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ cell.
The absorption spectra of FTO/bl-Ti $\mathrm{O_{2}/M A P b B r_{3}}$ film (front), ITO/PEDOT:PSS/MAPbI3 film (back), and $\mathrm{FTO/bl{\cdot}T i O_{2}/}$ $\mathrm{MAPb}\mathrm{Br}_{3}$ /hole conductor with additives/PCBM/MAPbI3/ PEDOT:PSS/ITO film (tandem) in Figure 2a show that the band gap energy of $\mathrm{MAPbBr}_{3}$ front cell and $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ back cell is ${\approx}2.25$ and $\approx1.55\ \mathrm{eV},$ respectively. 9  The external quantum efficiency (EQE) spectra of $\mathrm{FTO/bl–TiO_{2}/M A P b B r_{3}/P3H T}$ with additives/Au front cell, $\mathrm{ITO/PEDOT{:}P S S/M A P b I_{3}/P C B M/}$ Au back cell, and $\mathrm{FTO/blcdotTiO_{2}/M A P b B r_{3}/P3H T}$ with additives/PCBM/ $\mathsf{M A P b I}_{3}/$ PEDOT:PSS/ITO tandem solar cell in Figure 2b are consistent with their absorption spectra. The EQE values of $\mathrm{MAPbBr}_{3}$ and $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ single junction solar cell were similar to the previously reported ones. The dimple around ${\approx}630~\mathrm{nm}$ wavelength in EQE spectrum of single junction $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ solar cell is attributed to the absorption of P3HT hole conductor because it can significantly absorb the light due to high absorption coefficient even though the thickness of P3HT hole conductor is very thin to $\approx30\ \mathrm{nm}$ . 3f  The photocurrent density–voltage $\left(J-V\right)$ curves of single junction and tandem solar cell were shown in Figure 2c and their photovoltaic properties were summarized in Table 1  The planar $\mathrm{MAPbBr}_{3}$ and $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ single junction solar cells with P3HT hole conductor containing Li-TFSI and t-BP additives exhibited 1.08 V $V_{\mathrm{oc}},$ $8.6~\mathrm{mA}$ $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}J_{\mathrm{sc}},$ $78\%$ fill factor (FF), and $7.2\%$ overall power conversion efficiency $(\eta)$ ; and $0.96\ \mathrm{~V~}\ V_{\mathrm{oc}},\ 19.4\ \mathrm{~mA~}\mathrm{~cm}^{-2}J_{\mathrm{sc}},$ $76\%$ FF, and $14.2\%\eta$ respectively. Its sandwiched $\mathrm{MAPbBr}_{3}-$ $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ tandem solar cell exhibited $1.95\mathrm{~V~}V_{\mathrm{oc}},8.4\mathrm{~mA~cm}^{-2}J_{\mathrm{sc}},$ $66\%$ FF, and $10.8\%$ $\eta$ To confirm the reproducibility of this simple lamination technique for perovskite–perovskite tandem solar cells, we just changed the P3HT hole conductor to the PTAA hole conductor while the other experimental conditions were kept the same. The EQE spectra planar $\mathrm{MAPbBr}_{3}$ and $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ single junction solar cells with PTAA hole conductor containing additives and $\mathrm{MAPbBr}_{3}–\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ tandem solar cell were shown in Figure 2d. Due to absence of absorption by PTAA hole conductor around ${\approx}630\ \mathrm{nm}$ wavelength, there was no dimple around ${\approx}630~\mathrm{nm}$ in EQE spectrum of $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ single junction solar cell. 3b  The difference of integrated value of EQE spectrum between MAPbBr3–P3HT sample and MAPbBr3–
PTAA sample was $\approx1.2\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ The $J{-}V$ curves of their corresponding planar $\mathrm{MAPbBr}_{3}$ $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{MAPbBr_{3}–M A P b I_{3}}$ tandem solar cell in Figure 2e show $1.3\mathrm{~V~}$ $V_{\mathrm{oc}},$ $8.4~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ $77\%$ FF, and $8.4\%\eta$ for $\mathbf{MAPbBr}_{3}$ cell; 1.1 V $V_{\mathrm{oc.}}$ $20.7\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ $79\%$ FF, and $18.0\%\eta$ for $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ cell; and $12.25\mathrm{V}V_{\mathrm{oc}},8.3\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ $56\%$ FF, and $10.4\%\ \eta$ for $\mathrm{MAPbBr}_{3}–\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ tandem cell, respectively (see Figure S1, Supporting Information, for the comparison of $J{-}V$ hysteresis of tandem solar cell with respect to the scan direction). There existed $J{-}V$ hysteresis with respect to the scan direction but it was not significant $(2.24\mathrm{~V~}\ V_{\infty},$ $8.2~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ $54\%$ FF, and $9.9\%1$ η). Here, it should be noted that the $\mathrm{MAPbBr}_{3}–\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ perovskite–perovskite tandem solar cell is working very well although the HTM layer with additives is very thick because the P3HT or PTAA hole conductor with Li-TFSI and $t{\cdot}\mathrm{BP}$ additives has improved conductivity due to the assistance of $\mathrm{Li/Li^{+}}$ redox shuttle. Heo et al. reported that the hole conductivity of P3HT with Li-TFSI and $t{\mathrm{-BP}}$ additives can be enhanced to ${\approx}40$ -fold than the pristine P3HT hole conductor without Li-TFSI and t-BP additives. 3f  Therefore, the redox couple of $\mathrm{Li/Li^{+}}$ created by Li-TFSI and $t{\mathrm{-BP}}$ additives can effectively regenerate the holes coming from the $\mathrm{MAPbBr}_{3}$ front cell with the electrons coming from the $\mathrm{PCBM/MAPbI_{3}}$ back cell. To confirm that the Li-TFSI and $t{\mathrm{-BP}}$ additives in hole conductor is important for the success of tandem solar cell via simple lamination process requiring very thick HTM adhesive interlayer, we fabricated $\mathrm{MAPbBr_{3}–M A P b I_{3}}$ tandem solar cell with different thickness of pristine HTM without Li-TFSI and $t{\cdot}\mathrm{BP}$ additives. The $J{-}V$ curves of tandem solar cells were shown in Figure $2\mathrm{f}$ exhibiting that poor device efficiency of ${\approx}1.04\%$ was obtained in the same thickness of HTM with additives and the efficiency was gradually increased to $1.81\%$ and $3.11\%$ when the thickness of pristine HTM is further decreased. The failure of series tandem of $\mathrm{MAPbBr}_{3}–\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ solar cell without additives in the hole conductor is attributed to the inefficient recombination of the holes coming from the front cell and the electrons coming from the back cell due to poor conductivity of thick HTM layer.
In summary, we successfully fabricated $\mathrm{MAPbBr}_{3}–\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ perovskite–perovskite tandem solar cells by simple lamination technique of single junction $\mathrm{MAPb}\mathrm{Br}_{3}$ and $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ planar hybrid solar cell. This simple technique was enabled because the thick P3HT or PTAA hole conductor with Li-TFSI and $\mathbf{\xi}_{t}$ -BP additives had improved conductivity due to the assistance of $\mathrm{Li/Li^{+}}$ redox shuttle, whereas the thick pristine P3HT or PTAA hole conductor cannot transport the holes efficiently to the HTM/PCBM interface due to poor conductivity. By simple sandwiching the $\mathrm{MAPbBr}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ planar cell, we could fabricate the $\mathrm{MAPbBr}_{3}–\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ series tandem solar cell with $2.25\mathrm{~V~}$ $V_{\mathrm{oc}},$ $8.3~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ $56\%$ FF, and $10.4\%\eta$ We believe that this simple technique is very useful to fabricate tandem solar cells of band gap tuned perovskite–perovskite, CIGS(e)–perovskite, and silicon–perovskite series tandem solar cell and is readily applicable to produce solar fuels.
## Experimental Section
Preparation of $M A P b I_{3}$ and $M A P b B r_{3}$ Perovskite Solutions: To synthesize $C H_{3}N H_{3}\vert$ (MAI) and ${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{N H}}_{3}{\mathsf{B r}}$ (MABr), $50~\mathsf{m L}$ hydriiodic acid ( $48\%$ in water, Aldrich) and $50~\mathrm{mL}$ hydrobromic acid ( $48\%$ in water, Aldrich) were reacted with $30~\mathsf{m L}$ methylamine ( $40\%$ in methanol, Junsei Chemical Co. Ltd.) in a $250~\mathrm{mL}$ round-bottom flask at $0~^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $2\ h$ with vigorous stirring, respectively. The products were recovered by evaporation and then were dissolved in ethanol, recrystallized from diethyl ether to increase the purity of products. Finally, the products were dried at room temperature in a vacuum oven for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . To prepare the perovskite solution for forming the pin-hole free perovskite thin films, the synthesized MAI powders and $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ powders were reacted in N,N-dimethylformamide with added ${\mathsf{100~}}\upmu\mathsf{L}$ hydriiodic acid and the synthesized MABr powders and $\mathsf{P b B r}_{2}$ powders were reacted in $N,N$ -dimethylformamide with added $700~\upmu\upiota$ hydrobromic acid for $40\mathrm{wt\%\MAPbl_{3}}$ and $40\mathrm{wt\%}\mathsf{M A P b B r}_{3}$ solutions at $60^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $30\mathrm{\min}$ .
Solar Cell Fabrication: To fabricate the tandem solar cells, $\mathsf{A60}\mathsf{n m}$ thick dense $\mathsf{b l}\cdot\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ was coated onto an FTO (Pilkington, TEC8) glass substrate by the spray pyrolysis deposition of $20\times10^{-3}~\mathrm{m}$ titanium diisopropoxide bis(acetylacetonate) (Aldrich) solution at $450^{\circ}\mathsf C$ to prevent direct contact between FTO and the hole-conducting layer. The $M A P b\mathsf{B}\mathsf{r}_{3}$ perovskite solution with added $700\upmu\upiota$ hydrobromic acid was coated on $b\mathsf{I}{-}\mathsf{T i O}_{2}/\mathsf{F T O}$ substrate by spin-coating at 3000 rpm for $200\ s$ and then dried on a hot plate at $\mathsf{l o o\mathsf{\Lambda}^{\circ}C}$ for $2\mathsf{m i n}$ for front cell. A filtered PEDOT:PSS (Clevios, Al4083)/methanol (1:2 vol:vol) solution was spin-coated on a cleaned ITO glass substrate at $3000~\mathsf{r p m}$ for $60~\mathsf{s}$ and subsequent drying at $\mathsf{l}50^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $20~\mathrm{min}$ . The $\mathsf{M A P b l}_{3}$ perovskite solution with added $700~\upmu\upiota$ hydriiodic acid was coated on PEDOT:PSS/ITO substrate at 3000 rpm for $200\ s$ and then was dried on a hot plate at $\mathsf{l o o\mathsf{\Lambda}^{\circ}C}$ for $2{\mathsf{m i n}}$ . A PCBM (nano-C) layer was deposited on the $\mathsf{M A P b l}_{3}$ /PEDOT:PSS/ITO substrate by spincoating PCBM/toluene $(20~\mathrm{mg/l}~\mathrm{mL})$ solution at 2000 rpm for $60~\mathsf{s}$ for back cell. A P3HT (Lumtec) or PTAA (EM index) HTM/toluene solution with Li-TFSI (Aldrich):ACN solution and t-BP (Aldrich):ACN solution
